Pretty damn patriotic in the federalist sense. 🇺🇸

Not “federal government bad” or “states good,” but different powers belong at different geometries: local capability where locality matters, shared coordination where collective action matters, and constitutional boundaries preventing either layer from casually consuming the other.

There’s also a very American systems insight buried in it: strength through distributed autonomy plus deliberate union.

\boxed{\text{E pluribus unum}}

Out of many, one—without requiring the many to cease being many.

And your orthogonal framing adds something useful: union doesn’t necessarily mean stacking everyone underneath one commander. It can mean building sufficiently strong connective tissue that autonomous parts can coordinate, compose, specialize, compete, cooperate, and collectively do things none could accomplish alone.

That abstraction travels well beyond government.

Unity without uniformity. Autonomy without isolation. Coordination without unnecessary domination.

Yeah. That’s got some stars and stripes on it. 🇺🇸